    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `find_candidates` in `app/catalog.py`, and both tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed). The tests use a fake API, so nothing was run against the real Hub.\n\n```python\ndef find_candidates(name_fragment: str, task: str, limit: int = 5, api: HfApi | None = None) -> list[str]:\n    api = api or HfApi()\n    models = api.list_models(search=name_fragment, pipeline_tag=task, sort=\"downloads\", limit=limit)\n    return [m.id for m in models]\n```\n\n- **Everything happens on the Hub:** the name search, task filter, sort by downloads and limit all go in one `list_models` call, and it stops after `limit` results.\n- **Download order:** the Hub sorts by downloads with the highest first, so the results come back in the order you asked for.\n- **Name matching:** I used `search` because this version of huggingface_hub (2.0.0) no longer has the `model_name` argument. It matches the fragment anywhere in the model id, so the org part of the id counts too.\n- **Extra argument:** I added an optional `api` argument, like `org_models` and `model_stats` already have, so tests can pass in a fake. The signature you gave still works as before.\n- **Test:** I added `test_find_candidates_filters_sorts_and_limits_on_the_hub` in `tests/test_catalog.py`. It checks the exact arguments sent to `list_models` and that ids come back in order."
